            <ArticleTitle>Does Ankle Aspiration for Acute Ankle Fractures Result in Pain Relief? A Prospective Randomized Double-Blinded Placebo Controlled Trial.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>399-403</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/BOT.0000000000000314</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Does ankle aspiration help with pain control in patients with ankle fractures?</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective, double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled trial.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Level 1 Academic Medical Center.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS/PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Consecutive skeletally mature patients with ankle fractures.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Randomized between ankle aspiration and sham procedure.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASUREMENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Pain scores for 72 hours after injury and pain medicine usage.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Comparison between study subjects receiving ankle aspiration and sham procedure showed no significant differences in pain scores acutely in the emergency department or within 3 days after injury. There were also no statistically significant differences in pain medicine usage within 3 days after injury. Secondary outcomes, including lower leg volume, 6-month functional outcome scores, and complication rate, also showed no significant differences between subjects receiving aspiration and the sham procedure.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Aspiration of acute ankle fractures does not result in decreased pain scores or opioid usage after aspiration. Aspiration of acute ankle fractures does not provide measurable clinical benefit.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="LEVEL OF EVIDENCE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Therapeutic Level I. See Instructions for Authors for a complete description of levels of evidence.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
